oing it and they're  doing so in several different ways  one way is that you can actually turn  genes off and learn what what it was  that those genes were doing before you  turn them off by seeing  how an embryo breaks when it's trying to  develop  and we do that a lot with zebrafish and  with mice and so on  and the molecule that's used for that is  normally morpholinos  stretches of artificial rna that can  actually turn  genes off if you know the sequence of a  gene you can order a morpholino with  the anti-sequence to that gene and it  will silence that gene  and then another way to figure out what  different mutations do  how they affect an organism is to just  look at a bunch of different organisms  that have different traits you know  close relatives that have slightly  different traits or dramatically  different traits and then you look  at their genomes you sequence their  genomes you look for differences and you  correlate those differences  in genotype with differences in  phenotype  again genotype is the sequence of dna  or rna if it's like a virus or something  genotype is the sequence  of dna that an organism has the  phenotype  is the the structure of an organism once  it's developed  the really cool thing about dogs is that  there are so many different types of  breeds and they have so many different  traits  that you can actually just study  different dog breeds compare their  genomes with the genomes of other dog  breeds  and you can start to get a picture of  what mutations are causing their unique  traits  and to do this properly and to really  figure out the actual  you know causation instead of just some  random correlation  you know correlation isn't always  causation right to do this well you need  to look at lots of different dog breeds  try and follow single traits you know  there are lots of dogs that have flat  faces  there's pugs of course but there's also  boston terriers and  french bulldogs the skull that i have  this actually comes 