ey  can copy a chunk that's already been  copied and they make a strand of dna  that's too long  in other cases a slip strand mispairing  can cause a chunk of dna to be skipped  when being copied so it can make the  gene shorter than normal  these types of mutations are extremely  common when you have  a stretch of dna with tandem repeats  in its sequence what scientists found in  this 2004 paper called  molecular origins a rapid and continuous  morphological evolution what they found  is that any mutation that increases the  length of  q segment q in this gene relative  to segment a this causes the top of the  snout to grow  faster than normal in other words this  is exactly what we see  in the bull terrier because the top of  the snout is growing faster  than the bottom of the snout and check  this out  they actually found that bull terriers  have an extremely long q compared to a  segment in this gene and the run x2 gene  and  they went kind of back in time if you  look at photos of old  bull terriers you know if you go back  100 years or so  you'll see that they still have the  droopy face but it's not nearly as  extreme  and they actually found tissue preserved  from one of these old dogs with kind of  the intermediate version  of this trait and they sequence its  genome and sure enough  there was an intermediate version of  this gene here the ron x2 gene the  q was slightly longer than a but not  extremely longer as we find in modern  bull terriers this was a really cool  confirmation of this finding that  yeah this mutation the lengthening of  segment  q compared to segment a really does seem  to be what's causing  the fast growth on the top of the snout  versus the slow growth  on the bottom of the snout now as you  might expect the reverse  is also true if mutations lengthen the a  segment in relation to the q segment  the top of the snout will grow slower  than the bottom of the snout  and as a result the face will curl  upwards  now this mutation alone ca