lly important  mutation in our genomes and in the  genomes of dogs  there is a gene called smoc2 smock2  in flat-faced dogs this gene contains a  large insertion a retrotransposon  has been inserted into an intron in this  gene so an intron is a part of a gene  that's not normally  coded into protein it's just sitting  there in the middle and a  retrotransposon has been inserted in  there  retrotransposons for those who don't  know are chunks of dna that are able to  make copies of themselves and do sort of  a copy-paste  mechanism throughout the genome of an  organism so they code for a protein  that then makes a copy of that gene and  inserts it into other chunks of an  organism's  genome and a lot of these come from  viruses so this retrotransposon has been  inserted into the middle of an intron in  the middle of the  smock 2 gene several really careful  experiments show that this  insertion slows the expression of the  smok  2 gene it makes it so that during  embryological development  less of the protein coded for by this  gene is produced  and as an end result you get really flat  faces  this mutation alone was responsible for  more flatness  in the face and any other mutation we  found so far  if it is the only mutation that's  inherited and this we were able to  figure out by studying different cross  breeds if a crossbreed has  this mutation and not the other two  mutations  it still has a pretty dang flat face  it's not as flat as if it has all three  of these things these mutations work  in kind of an additive manner but with  this mutation alone they have a very  very flat face so let me just kind of  put this all into perspective here  we have discovered we scientists have  discovered there are three  different types of mutations that can  cause  a flat nose to occur in dogs we've got a  slippage mutation  we've got a point mutation and we have a  an insertion mutation these three very  very different types of mutations  completely different origins  th